<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The resurrection is the claim on which the Christian reading of Jesus rests, and the four accounts of it are the part of the gospels in which the four writers are most clearly working without a common written source: Mark ends before any appearance, and Matthew, Luke and John each supply their own. The table sets the accounts side by side, detail by detail, with chapter and verse, and records only what each text says. Where a cell says “none”, the gospel is silent on that detail; where two cells differ, the texts differ. Interpretation is kept out of the table and placed in the notes below it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:table {"hasFixedLayout":false,"align":"wide","className":"is-style-discrepancy"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-table alignwide is-style-discrepancy"><table><thead><tr><th>Detail</th><th>Mark</th><th>Matthew</th><th>Luke</th><th>John</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who goes to the tomb</td><td>Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, Salome <code>16:1</code></td><td>Mary Magdalene and the other Mary <code>28:1</code></td><td>Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Mary the mother of James, and other women <code>24:10</code></td><td>Mary Magdalene <code>20:1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>Very early, after sunrise <code>16:2</code></td><td>Toward dawn <code>28:1</code></td><td>At early dawn <code>24:1</code></td><td>While it was still dark <code>20:1</code></td></tr><tr><td>The stone</td><td>Already rolled back <code>16:4</code></td><td>An angel descends and rolls it back <code>28:2</code></td><td>Found rolled away <code>24:2</code></td><td>Found taken away <code>20:1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Who is there</td><td>A young man in white, sitting inside <code>16:5</code></td><td>An angel, sitting on the stone <code>28:2-5</code></td><td>Two men in dazzling clothes, standing <code>24:4</code></td><td>Two angels, seen later by Mary <code>20:12</code></td></tr><tr><td>The women's response</td><td>Flee and tell no one, for they were afraid <code>16:8</code></td><td>Run to tell the disciples <code>28:8</code></td><td>Tell the eleven and the rest <code>24:9</code></td><td>Mary runs to Peter and the beloved disciple <code>20:2</code></td></tr><tr><td>First appearance</td><td>None in the earliest manuscripts; the longer ending gives Mary Magdalene <code>16:9</code></td><td>The women, on the way <code>28:9</code></td><td>Two disciples on the Emmaus road <code>24:13-31</code></td><td>Mary Magdalene, at the tomb <code>20:14</code></td></tr><tr><td>Where the disciples see him</td><td>Galilee, promised <code>16:7</code></td><td>Galilee, on a mountain <code>28:16</code></td><td>Jerusalem; told to stay in the city <code>24:33-49</code></td><td>Jerusalem <code>20:19</code>; later Galilee <code>21:1</code></td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">References are to chapter and verse in each gospel. Mark 16:9-20 is absent from Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus and is widely held to be a later addition (scholarly consensus).</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:table -->
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Reading the table</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>The women.</strong> Each gospel names a different group, from one woman in John to several in Luke. Mary Magdalene is the only name in all four. Matthew and Luke, who both copy Mark here, each changed his list.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>The stone and the messenger.</strong> In Mark the stone is already rolled back and a young man sits inside; in Matthew an angel descends in an earthquake, rolls it back and sits on it, before the women’s eyes; in Luke there are two men; in John, two angels, seen only by Mary and only on her second visit. A young man has become an angel, and one has become two, within twenty years of Mark.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>The message.</strong> In Mark and Matthew the women are told to send the disciples to Galilee, where Jesus will meet them. In Luke the same words are rewritten to refer back to what Jesus said while he was in Galilee, and the disciples are told to stay in Jerusalem (24:49). The two instructions cannot both have been given.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>What the women did.</strong> Mark’s oldest text ends with the women fleeing and saying nothing to anyone (16:8). Matthew, Luke and John have them run to tell the disciples. The writer who added the longer ending to Mark (16:9-20) did so because Mark, as it stood, had no one hear the news.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Where he appeared.</strong> Matthew’s appearances are in Galilee, on a mountain, where some doubted (28:16-17). Luke’s are all in and around Jerusalem on one day, and he ascends that evening (24:50-51); Acts, by the same author, makes it forty days (1:3). John has Jerusalem and then, in the appended chapter 21, the Sea of Tiberias. Mark, in the oldest manuscripts, has none. A reader who follows Matthew will have the disciples in Galilee when Luke has them under orders not to leave the city.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>What follows.</strong> These are not variations of emphasis. They are different answers to the questions who went, what they saw, what they were told, what they did and where he was seen, given by four writers who were not there, two of whom had the first writer’s account in front of them and changed it. The historians’ consensus is that the disciples believed they had seen him; what the table shows is that the churches did not possess one account of how. The Qur’an’s statement on the matter, and this website’s position, are on <a href="/quran/raised/">the end of his earthly mission</a>; the measure applied to these accounts in the gospel is on <a href="/method/">the measure</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"small"} -->
<p class="has-small-font-size">Sources: Raymond Brown, <em>The Death of the Messiah</em>; Bart Ehrman, <em>Jesus, Interrupted</em>, ch. 2; Bruce Metzger, <em>A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament</em>, on Mark 16:9-20.</p>
